Step 1: 1. Finding the Covalent Bond Length: In chemistry, the covalent bond length is defined as the average distance between the centers of the nuclei of two bonded atoms in a molecule. When atoms $A$ and $B$ form a single covalent bond, we can approximate the distance between their nuclei by simply adding their individual covalent radii. Therefore, if the covalent radius of atom $A$ is $r_A$ and the covalent radius of atom $B$ is $r_B$, the internuclear distance, which is the bond length of the $AB$ molecule, is: $$ \text{Bond Length} = r_A + r_B $$ 2. Finding the Total Length of the AB Molecule: To find the total length of the entire molecule, we need to consider the full physical extent of both atoms. Imagine the two atoms as spheres placed side by side on a straight axis. The center of atom $A$ to its outermost left edge is equal to its radius, $r_A$. The distance from the center of atom $A$ to the center of atom $B$ is the bond length, which we already established is $(r_A + r_B)$. The center of atom $B$ to its outermost right edge is equal to its radius, $r_B$. The total length of the molecule is the distance from the outermost left edge of atom $A$ to the outermost right edge of atom $B$. We can find this by adding the three segments together: $$ \text{Total Length} = (\text{radius of }
A) + (\text{internuclear distance}) + (\text{radius of }
B) $$ $$ \text{Total Length} = r_A + (r_A + r_
B) + r_B $$ $$ \text{Total Length} = 2r_A + 2r_B $$ $$ \text{Total Length} = 2(r_A + r_
B) $$ So, the covalent bond length is $(r_A + r_B)$ and the total length of the full $AB$ molecule is $2(r_A + r_B)$. Comparing our findings with the given options, this corresponds perfectly to Option
A. Correct Answer: Option A $\left(\mathrm{r}_{\mathrm{A}}+\mathrm{r}_{\mathrm{B}}\right), 2\left(\mathrm{r}_{\mathrm{A}}+\mathrm{r}_{\mathrm{B}}\right)$

